------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2509.22134v1] Bridging Draft Policy Misalignment: Group Tree Optimization for Speculative Decoding (https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22134v1)\nciteturn3361view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22134v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 161\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 26 Sep 2025 (this version), latest version 28 Feb 2026 (cite5†v2 )]\nL9: # Title:Bridging Draft Policy Misalignment: Group Tree Optimization for Speculative Decoding\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite6†Shijing Hu , cite7†Jingyang Li , cite8†Zhihui Lu , cite9†Pan Zhou L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Bridging Draft Policy Misalignment: Group Tree Optimization for Speculative Decoding, by Shijing Hu and 2 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite10†View PDF cite11†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Speculative decoding accelerates large language model (LLM) inference by letting a lightweight draft model propose multiple tokens that the target model verifies in parallel. Yet existing training objectives optimize only a single greedy draft path, while decoding follows a tree policy that re-ranks and verifies multiple branches. This draft policy misalignment limits achievable speedups.\nL17: We introduce Group Tree Optimization (GTO), which aligns training with the decoding-time tree policy through two components: (i) Draft Tree Reward, a sampling-free objective equal to the expected acceptance length of the draft tree under the target model, directly measuring decoding performance; (ii) Group-based Draft Policy Training, a stable optimization scheme that contrasts trees from the current and a frozen reference draft model, forming debiased group-standardized advantages and applying a PPO-style surrogate along the longest accepted sequence for robust updates.\nL18: We further prove that increasing our Draft Tree Reward provably improves acceptance length and speedup. Across dialogue (MT-Bench), code (HumanEval), and math (GSM8K), and multiple LLMs (e.g., LLaMA-3.1-8B, LLaMA-3.3-70B, Vicuna-1.3-13B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill-LLaMA-8B), GTO increases acceptance length by 7.4% and yields an additional 7.7% speedup over prior state-of-the-art EAGLE-3. By bridging draft policy misalignment, GTO offers a practical, general solution for efficient LLM inference.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite12†arXiv:2509.22134 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite13†arXiv:2509.22134v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite14†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.22134†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Shijing Hu [cite15†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Fri, 26 Sep 2025 09:55:35 UTC (179 KB)\nL27: cite5†[v2] Sat, 28 Feb 2026 08:57:38 UTC (182 KB)\nL28: \nL29: Full-text links:\nL30: \nL31: ## Access Paper:\nL32: \nL33: View a PDF of the paper titled Bridging Draft Policy Misalignment: Group Tree Optimization for Speculative Decoding, by Shijing Hu and 2 other authors\nL34: \nL35:   * cite10†View PDF L36:   * cite11†HTML (experimental) L37:   * cite16†TeX Source L38: \nL39: cite17†view license L40: ### Current browse context:\nL41: \nL42: cs.CL\nL43: \nL44: cite18†< prev |   cite19†next > L45: \nL46: cite20†new | cite21†recent | cite22†2025-09 L47: \nL48: Change to browse by:\nL49: \nL50: cite23†cs L51: cite24†cs.AI L52: \nL53: ### References & Citations\nL54: \nL55:   * cite25†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L56:   * cite26†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L57:   * cite27†Semantic Scholar†